# Ferromark v4

**Markdown at native speed.**

Ferromark turns Markdown into HTML for documentation sites, publishing tools,
and applications. A fast Rust engine with an arena-allocated document tree,
CommonMark correctness, and practical publishing features. Ferromark Flavored
Markdown adds opt-in syntax for richer documents. Use the Rust crate or its
native Node.js bindings.

<!-- x-release-please-start-version -->

[Documentation](https://ferromark.dev/) ·
[Rust API](https://docs.rs/ferromark/4.0.0/ferromark/) ·
[Node.js package](node/ferromark/README.md)

<!-- x-release-please-end-version -->

**V4 changes code-engine setup and hook contracts.** Plain Markdown rendering
keeps its defaults. Read the [v3-to-v4 upgrade guide](docs/migration-v4.md) and
[changelog](CHANGELOG.md). For earlier upgrades, follow the
[v1-to-v2](docs/migration-v2.md) and [v2-to-v3](docs/migration-v3.md) guides first.

## Why Ferromark

A Markdown engine earns its place when the documents get complicated.
Nested lists. Reference links. Tables with real content. The page that looked
fine until an author added one more footnote.

- **Keep the core focused.** Parse Markdown and emit HTML or JSX. Your application
  chooses translation, site assembly, and the highlighter connected through
  rendering hooks.
- **Build correctness in.** The explicit CommonMark profile agrees with all
  652 specification examples. Differential checks against cmark and cmark-gfm
  expose edge cases and keep fixes reproducible.
- **Cover everyday publishing needs.** Front matter, heading IDs, footnotes,
  callouts, and inline tables of contents support real documents. Table classes,
  captions, merged cells, and named columns give CSS control over layout.
- **Write richer Markdown.** Ferromark Flavored Markdown adds image captions,
  quote attributions, attributes and inline spans, inserted text, and technical
  abbreviations. Each feature is opt-in, so unused syntax stays out of the way.
- **Keep rendering fast.** Arena allocation, reusable buffers, specialized byte
  scans, and SIMD reduce parser and renderer work. You can inspect or transform
  the document tree before rendering it.

<!-- native-benchmarks -->
Ferromark leads every measured library in our 57-document comparison on Linux
x86-64 and macOS arm64. CommonMark and GFM, plus opt-in publishing features for
richer documents. Extra syntax adds parsing work.

Measured 2026-10-01, using the recorded profiles, fresh calls and all 57 frozen
inputs, including different outputs. Node.js measurements include native binding
overhead. Syntax and API contracts differ.
[Machines, versions, methods, and raw data](https://ferromark.dev/guide/benchmarks).
<!-- /native-benchmarks -->

[Syntax and features](https://ferromark.dev/guide/features),
[Ferromark Flavored Markdown](https://ferromark.dev/guide/ffm),
[correctness and limits](https://ferromark.dev/guide/correctness),
and [measured comparisons](https://ferromark.dev/guide/benchmarks)
explain the details and trade-offs.

## Quick start

### Rust

```sh
cargo add ferromark
```

```rust
let html = ferromark::to_html("Hello, **world**!").unwrap();
assert_eq!(html, "<p>Hello, <strong>world</strong>!</p>\n");
```

Rust requires **1.95 or newer**. The helpers return `Result`. Rust defaults
preserve raw HTML; set `HtmlRendererOptions::sanitize` to `true` for untrusted
input. The [Rust guide](https://ferromark.dev/rust/getting-started)
covers configuration, ownership, and AST access through `ferromark`.

### Node.js

```sh
npm install ferromark
```

```js
import { toHtml } from 'ferromark'

const html = toHtml('Hello, **world**!')
```

Requires **Node.js 22.12 or newer**. Node defaults escape raw HTML and filter
unsafe URL schemes. The [Node.js guide](https://ferromark.dev/node/getting-started)
covers metadata, Buffers, reusable renderers, and highlighters.

### JSX and MDX

`compileJsx(source, { format: "mdx" })` returns a JSX body and metadata.
With `output: "module"` it returns a complete MDX module and source map instead.
Your application compiles JSX and supplies components. Ferromark adds no React
imports. JSX is a trusted program; HTML APIs keep their existing trust policies.

`compileJsx` accepts the same code hooks as the HTML APIs. See the
[Node JSX guide](docs/jsx-node.md) and [Rust renderer contract](docs/jsx-renderer.md).

### Syntax highlighting and Mermaid diagrams

Ferriki highlights code and Ferrigura renders Mermaid as static SVG. Both use
the same code-block hooks: the `ferriki` and `ferrigura` features in Rust, or
`@ferriki/core` and `@ferrigura/core` passed to the Node renderer. Without them,
Ferromark contains no engine.
[Highlighting and diagrams guide](https://ferromark.dev/guide/code-rendering).

### Document analysis

Rust and Node offer
[reference collection](docs/references.md),
[text extraction](docs/text-extraction.md), and
[static Markdown projection](docs/markdown-projection.md).
[Examples and release availability](https://ferromark.dev/guide/document-analysis).

